  "diagnosis_text": "Diagnosis: the failure is caused by an incorrect high-priority flow on **s1**, not by a down physical link.\n\n- `h1 \u2192 h5` ping: **100% loss**.\n- Local attachment/access paths work:\n  - `h1 \u2194 h2`: successful\n  - `h5 \u2194 h6`: successful\n- Adjacent branch connectivity works:\n  - `h1 \u2192 h3` via `s4\u2013s2\u2013s5`: successful\n  - `h5 \u2192 h7` via `s6\u2013s3\u2013s7`: successful\n- All observed inter-switch links, including **s1 port 2 \u2194 s3 port 1**, are ACTIVE and enabled; the two-second measurement showed no drops or errors.\n\nThe normal route from h1 to h5 is:\n\n`h1 \u2014 s4 \u2014 s2 \u2014 s1 \u2014 s3 \u2014 s6 \u2014 h5`\n\nAt **s1**, traffic arriving from s2 should leave **port 2** toward s3. However, s1 has this higher-priority flow:\n\n- match: Ethernet source `00:00:00:00:00:01` (h1), destination `00:00:00:00:00:05` (h5)\n- priority: **50000**\n- action: output **port 1** (back toward s2)\n\nThis overrides the baseline h5 forwarding entry, which correctly selects **port 2** toward s3. The bad rule has already matched packets, so it directly explains the failed h1\u2192h5 direction.\n\n**Localisation:** s1 forwarding plane; the erroneous flow is the experiment-app rule for h1-to-h5, outputting port 1 instead of port 2. The reverse direction may still be routable, but it was not required to establish this one-way fault.",
